to-paul-graham-source : List Word
to-paul-graham-source = lines-of (cons "# Paul Graham"
  (cons ""
  (cons "Paul, this is written to you because you kept John McCarthy's idea alive in public for a generation of programmers who would otherwise have learned only the C model. Everything here that descends from McCarthy credits him in full, and everything that descends from your reading of him credits you in full. Each claim below about Avik Jain's work links to the block of his paper, *hyperactivity*, Part I, that states it. Read it looking for the best in it."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## What you saw that the field did not"
  (cons ""
  (cons "In The Roots of Lisp you retraced McCarthy's 1960 paper and stated its point plainly: a handful of simple operators and a notation for functions are enough to build a whole language, one structure serves for both code and data, and the language can be written in itself. You also drew the map most of the field still refuses to draw: two clean models of programming, C and Lisp, with newer languages drifting toward Lisp one feature at a time."
  (cons ""
  (cons "In Beating the Averages (2001) you gave the reason the drift is slow: programmers judge languages from inside the one they use, so the Blub programmer looks up and sees only strangeness. Viaweb was the evidence: written in Lisp, with roughly a fifth to a quarter of the editor's code being macros, programs that write programs."
  (cons ""
  (cons "Then in 2019 you published Bel, a Lisp specified in itself, and asked what happens if, instead of switching from the formal phase to the implementation phase as soon as possible, you delay that switch as long as possible. You said plainly that Bel, like the Lisp of the 1960 paper, is not yet a language for programming computers. These three pieces are undervalued: the field reads them as essays by an investor. They are a correct diagnosis of computing: the formal and the implemented came apart early, and the industry standardized the split."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## What hyperactivity does with that question"
  (cons ""
  (cons "In Avik's words, hyperactivity is “a language and a computational model of reality i.e. wolfram's ruliad executing on silicon”. The paper places Lisp reflection among the foundational antecedents of a language that operates on its own descriptions"
  (cons (cite s6-p2 "(§6)")
  (cons ". Its core is the CCHM cubical calculus with clock-indexed guarded computation"
  (cons (cite s1-1-p4 "(§1.1)")
  (cons ". A routine, the requirement it meets and the reasoning that establishes the requirement are terms of one language"
  (cons (cite s1-p25 "(1.5)")
  (cons "; expressing that reasoning in the language of the construction preserves it for further use instead of translating it into a separate verification model"
  (cons (cite s1-p26 "(§1)")
  (cons ". That is Bel's delayed switch taken to its end: the formal phase does not end, because the reasoning is kept in the same language as the program."
  (cons ""
  (cons "McCarthy made code and data one structure. The paper adds two things to it."
  (cons ""
  (cons "- **Meaning.** Equality is computational: an equivalence of types gives a path, and transport along it carries dependent constructions across"
  (cons (cite s1-1-p5 "(§1.1)")
  (cons ". Your macros rewrite programs; here the specializer returns, beside the new code, a code for its justification, an explicit path between the interpreted terms when a theorem rewrite was applied"
  (cons (cite s6-2-p6 "(§6.2)")
  (cons ". A program that writes programs returns the evidence that the meaning was kept."
  (cons "- **Identity.** Definitions are content-addressed objects with their typed syntax and dependency references; a name is a view, so renaming does not rewrite the computation"
  (cons (cite s10-8-p2 "(§10.8)")
  (cons ". The checker is itself a term of the language, and the specializer's results are admitted through the same checking relation as ordinary definitions"
  (cons (cite s1-2-p2 "(§1.2)")
  (cons ": metacircular in the sense of Bel's source being Bel."
  (cons ""
  (cons "Your Blub argument applies one level up. A Lisp programmer looking up at a language where a requirement and its proof travel with the program may see only ceremony. What looks like complication from below is power seen from above. When a definition changes, a derived result is retained only if its dependency certificate still validates, and an old proof does not silently become a proof about the new program"
  (cons (cite s10-11-p4 "(§10.11)")
  (cons (cite s10-11-p6 "(§10.11)")
  (cons "."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## Why you in particular"
  (cons ""
  (cons "How to Do Great Work (July 2023) reads as a description of how this work was made: fields look smooth from a distance and are full of gaps near the frontier, outsiders find things because they do not share the field's assumptions, and early versions of important things get dismissed as toys. Avik studied EECS at Berkeley, then spent ten years on one product outside academic publishing. Before that, at Citadel Securities, he and Gašper Ažman built a language for arbitrary protocols that generated optimal template-only C++ parser libraries: programs that write programs, where speed is measured in nanoseconds. Gašper gets full credit for that work."
  (cons ""
  (cons "Avik's words about the class of people this work is easiest to show: “a class of humans oriented the opposite way without any deep expertise but deep receptivity to expertise”. You have deep expertise in exactly the lineage this work continues, and the receptivity of someone who has spent twenty years listening to people describe things that did not exist yet."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## What is here for you"
  (cons ""
  (cons "The 1960 paper's operators, your retelling of them and Bel's axioms all sit in hyperactivity's lineage, each credited as the source it is. What it offers you is the end of the line of thought you have defended since the 1990s: a language whose formal phase never ends, because its implementation carries its reasoning."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## Sources"
  (cons ""
  (cons "- Paul Graham, The Roots of Lisp, https://paulgraham.com/rootsoflisp.html"
  (cons "- Paul Graham, Beating the Averages, April 2001, revised April 2003, https://paulgraham.com/avg.html"
  (cons "- Paul Graham, Bel, October 2019, https://paulgraham.com/bel.html"
  (cons "- Paul Graham, How to Do Great Work, July 2023, https://paulgraham.com/greatwork.html"
  (cons "- John McCarthy, Recursive Functions of Symbolic Expressions and Their Computation by Machine, Part I, 1960, https://www.cl.cam.ac.uk/teaching/1617/ConceptsPL/jmc.pdf"
  (cons "- Avik Jain, hyperactivity, Part I, the blocks linked above"
  nil))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))
